  "title": "Load Balancing",
  "category": "Spring Framework",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "what-is-load-balancing",
      "heading": "What is Load Balancing?",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Load Balancing is the process of distributing incoming network traffic or requests evenly across multiple instances of a service to ensure no single instance becomes overwhelmed, thereby improving availability and responsiveness."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Microservices/Load Balancing/Load%20Balancing/image.png",
          "alt": "image.png",
          "originalPath": "Load%20Balancing/image.png"
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 31,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "examples-of-load-balancers",
          "heading": "Examples of Load Balancers",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Type",
                "Examples"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Hardware Load Balancers",
                  "F5 BIG-IP, Cisco Catalyst, Barracuda, Coytepoint"
                ],
                [
                  "Software Load Balancers",
                  "HAProxy, NGINX, mod_athena, Varnish"
                ],
                [
                  "Spring Cloud Client-Side Load Balancers",
                  "Ribbon, Spring Cloud Load Balancer"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 28,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "scenario-infytel-offer-and-scaling",
          "heading": "Scenario: Infytel Offer and Scaling",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Infytel announces a limited-time offer: customers with 5 friends get a 5% discount.",
                "This causes a surge in requests to the infytel-friend-family microservice.",
                "To handle the increased load, instances of infytel-friend-family are scaled from 1 to 2.",
                "Problem: How to distribute requests evenly between the two instances?",
                "Goal: Ensure requests from infytel-customer service are load balanced across the two friend-family instances."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 61,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "server-side-load-balancing",
      "heading": "Server-Side Load Balancing",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definition",
          "heading": "Definition:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In server-side load balancing, a dedicated load balancer receives all requests from clients and forwards each request to one of the available service instances."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 24,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "how-it-works",
          "heading": "How it works:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "The client sends requests to the load balancer’s endpoint.",
                "The load balancer decides which service instance handles each request.",
                "Typically implemented via hardware or software load balancers."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 27,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "example-setup",
          "heading": "Example Setup:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Hardware load balancer like F5 BIG-IP, Cisco Catalyst, Barracuda, or Coytepoint sits in front of microservice instances.",
                "Client (infytel-customer) sends all requests to the load balancer."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 26,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "advantages",
          "heading": "Advantages:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Centralized control over request routing.",
                "Often comes with advanced features (health checks, SSL termination)."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 14,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "problems-with-server-side-load-balancing",
          "heading": "Problems with Server-Side Load Balancing:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Single point of failure: If the load balancer fails, all access to the microservice is lost.",
                "Operational overhead: Managing hundreds of dedicated load balancers (one per microservice) is complex.",
                "Increased latency: Requests pass through the load balancer before reaching the microservice (two network hops)."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 44,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "client-side-load-balancing",
      "heading": "Client-Side Load Balancing",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [],
      "wordCount": 0,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "definition",
          "heading": "Definition:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Client-side load balancing moves the responsibility of load balancing from a central load balancer to the client itself. The client decides which service instance to call based on a list of available instances."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 33,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "how-it-works",
          "heading": "How it works:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "The client knows the list of available service instances.",
                "The client selects one instance for each request based on a load balancing algorithm.",
                "Typically implemented with software load balancer libraries embedded in the client."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 34,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "advantages",
          "heading": "Advantages:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Removes the single point of failure (no centralized load balancer).",
                "Reduces network latency (direct calls from client to service instance).",
                "Easier to scale and manage in microservice ecosystems."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 28,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "downsides",
          "heading": "Downsides:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Load balancing logic mixes with application code.",
                "Client must maintain knowledge of available service instances."
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 15,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "how-to-enable-ribbon-in-a-microservice",
      "heading": "How to Enable Ribbon in a Microservice",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Add Dependency in pom.xml:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "xml",
          "code": "<dependency>\n    <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>\n    <artifactId>spring-cloud-starter-netflix-ribbon</artifactId>\n</dependency>",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Create a Configuration Class:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "@Bean\n@LoadBalanced\npublic RestTemplate restTemplate() {\n    return new RestTemplate();\n}",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "@LoadBalanced tells Spring to use Ribbon’s load balancing for RestTemplate."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Autowire RestTemplate:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "@Autowired\nprivate RestTemplate template;\n",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Add Ribbon Client Annotation on Controller:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "@RibbonClient(name = \"custribbon\")\n@RestController\npublic class CustomerController { ... }\n",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Configure Ribbon Servers in application.properties:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "",
          "code": "custribbon.ribbon.eureka.enabled=false\ncustribbon.ribbon.listOfServers=http://localhost:8001,http://localhost:8002",
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "This config tells Ribbon the list of service instances to balance load across (two friend-family instances running on ports 8001 and 8002)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Use RestTemplate to Call Friend-Family Service:"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "language": "java",
          "code": "List<Long> friends = template.getForObject(\"http://custribbon/customers/\" + phoneNo + \"/friends\", List.class);",
          "runnable": false
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 59,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "running-multiple-instances",
          "heading": "Running Multiple Instances",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Run the second instance of FriendFamilyMS on a different port:"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "bash",
              "code": "java -jar -Dserver.port=9797 target/FriendFamilyMS-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT-exec.jar",
              "runnable": false
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 10,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ribbon-default-behavior-and-configuration",
      "heading": "Ribbon Default Behavior and Configuration",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "By default, Ribbon uses NoOpPing strategy which assumes all services are always up.",
            "This can cause Ribbon to send requests to instances that are down.",
            "You can configure different Ping strategies to improve health check behavior.",
            "Default load balancing algorithm: Round Robin (requests distributed evenly).",
            "Can configure other load balancing rules (e.g., Random, Weighted)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Microservices/Load Balancing/Load%20Balancing/image%201.png",
          "alt": "image.png",
          "originalPath": "Load%20Balancing/image%201.png"
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 54,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "example-configuration-for-load-balancing-strategy",
          "heading": "Example configuration for load balancing strategy:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "",
              "code": "custribbon.default.NFLoadBalancerRuleClassName=com.netflix.loadbalancer.RandomRule",
              "runnable": false
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 0,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
